{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794570724592,"sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","part":{"id":"prt_1d4c024f0001xXYsNO5semc80s","sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","messageID":"msg_1d4c013d4001oJj62JjTHjlmlx","type":"step-start","snapshot":"f0f67d8a3569c706c7d3a3779c0796721d0fd625"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794570728348,"sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4c02fee002mt2BPXz42nv4v0","sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","messageID":"msg_1d4c013d4001oJj62JjTHjlmlx","type":"tool","id":"call_b26b083a2cf04dfb987848ea","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research; wc -l .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md; echo \"=== hub chapter ===\"; cat .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md"},"output":"     360 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md\n     124 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md\n     484 total\n=== hub chapter ===\n---\ntitle: \"NC-011 Hub Recommendation Names Its Slash Command\"\ndescription: \"Manual validation that advisor_recommend adds the routed mode's slash command to a hub recommendation, holds a deep-loop command back until the prompt asks for depth and does not report a command row and its owner as a tie.\"\nversion: 1.0.0.0\nid: NC-011\ncategory: native_cli_tools\nstage: routing\n---\n\n# NC-011 Hub Recommendation Names Its Slash Command\n\nPrompt: Manual validation that advisor_recommend adds the routed mode's slash command to a hub recommendation, holds a deep-loop command back until the prompt asks for depth and does not report a command row and its owner as a tie.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nA hub skill such as `sk-doc` used to come back as a bare skill id. The reader then had to work out which slash command runs the mode the hub picked. When a hub's compiled route picks exactly one mode, the recommendation now carries a `command` field with that mode's slash command.\n\nThis scenario checks three rules with four real prompts:\n\n1. A plain authoring prompt for `sk-doc` returns `command: \"/create:readme\"`.\n2. A `system-deep-loop` command is attached only when the prompt asks for depth. A prompt that names the topic gets the same route and no command.\n3. A command row and the skill that owns it are one answer, so their near tie does not set `ambiguous`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n\n- Objective: Confirm the `command` field on a single-mode hub route, the deep-loop effort gate and the same-owner tie rule.\n- Real user request: `When the advisor picks sk-doc for my README, does it tell me which command to type?`\n- Prompt: `Run advisor_recommend on four prompts and check which recommendations carry a slash command and whether any result is marked ambiguous.`\n- Expected execution process: Run the four commands below from the repository root and read the fields named under Expected.\n- Expected signals: `/create:readme` on `sk-doc`, no `command` key on the topic-only deep-loop prompt, `/deep:research` on the same prompt with `in depth` added, and `ambiguous: false` on `/speckit:save`.\n- Desired user-visible outcome: The reader learns which command to type for a hub's mode, and is never offered a long deep-loop run they did not ask for.\n- Pass/fail: PASS if all four results match the expected signals. FAIL if any command is missing, extra or wrong, or if `/speckit:save` is marked ambiguous.\n\n---\n\n## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n\n### Prompt\n\n- Prompt: `Run advisor_recommend on four prompts and check which recommendations carry a slash command and whether any result is marked ambiguous.`\n\n### Commands\n\n1. `bash: mkdir -p /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook`\n2. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"create a readme for the scripts folder\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-011-readme.json`\n3. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"research loop on incremental indexing approaches\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-011-topic.json`\n4. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"research loop on incremental indexing approaches in depth\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-011-depth.json`\n5. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"/speckit:save\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-011-owner.json`\n6. In each file read `status`, `data.ambiguous` and, for each entry in `data.recommendations`, the `skillId`, `score`, `confidence`, `compiledRoute.targets[0].workflowMode` and `command`.\n\n### Exact Prompt Table\n\n| Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| NC-011 | Hub recommendation names its slash command | Confirm the `command` field, the deep-loop effort gate and the same-owner tie rule | `Run advisor_recommend on four prompts and check which recommendations carry a slash command and whether any result is marked ambiguous.` | Commands 1 to 6 above | `/create:readme` on `sk-doc`. No `command` on the topic prompt. `/deep:research` on the depth prompt. `ambiguous: false` on `/speckit:save` | The four JSON files plus the terminal transcript | PASS if all four results match. FAIL otherwise | See Failure Triage |\n\n### Expected\n\n- Every envelope has `status: \"ok\"` and the shell exit code is `0`.\n- `nc-011-readme.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc`. Its `compiledRoute` has `action: \"route\"`, `selectionKind: \"single\"` and one target with `workflowMode: \"sk-create-readme\"`. It carries `command: \"/create:readme\"`.\n- `nc-011-topic.json`: the first recommendation is `system-deep-loop` with a single route to the `research` mode. The recommendation has no `command` key, because the prompt names the topic and asks for no depth.\n- `nc-011-depth.json`: the same skill and the same `research` route, now with `command: \"/deep:research\"`. The words `in depth` are on the effort list, so they unlock the command.\n- `nc-011-owner.json`: `system-spec-kit` and `command-speckit-save` both rank, with scores less than 0.05 apart. `data.ambiguous` is `false`, because `command-speckit-save` belongs to `system-spec-kit`.\n\n### Evidence\n\nKeep the four JSON files and the terminal transcript. Reduced to one line per recommendation, a passing run reads like this:\n\n```text\ncreate a readme for the scripts folder\n  status=ok ambiguous=false\n  sk-doc score=0.78436 confidence=0.9416 route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=/create:readme\nresearch loop on incremental indexing approaches\n  status=ok ambiguous=false\n  system-deep-loop score=0.675806 confidence=0.8832 route=route/single/research command=(absent)\nresearch loop on incremental indexing approaches in depth\n  status=ok ambiguous=false\n  system-deep-loop score=0.65573 confidence=0.8725 route=route/single/research command=/deep:research\n/speckit:save\n  status=ok ambiguous=false\n  system-spec-kit score=0.738163 confidence=0.9168 command=(absent)\n  command-speckit-save score=0.7 confidence=0.8962 command=(absent)\n```\n\nThe scores can move after an index rebuild. The commands, the routes and the `ambiguous` value are the checked signals.\n\n### Pass / Fail\n\n- **Pass**: all four results match Expected.\n- **Fail**: `sk-doc` has no `command` or a different one, the topic prompt carries a command, the depth prompt does not carry `/deep:research`, or `/speckit:save` returns `ambiguous: true`.\n\n### Failure Triage\n\n1. `sk-doc` has no `compiledRoute` at all: run `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs`. A `stale-manifest` line for `sk-doc` means the hub serves legacy routing until its manifest is refreshed, and legacy routing names no command.\n2. A route exists but no command: check that the routed mode has a row in `MODE_COMMANDS` in `runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, and that the runtime build is current.\n3. The topic prompt carries a deep-loop command: compare the prompt with `DEPTH_WORDING` in `runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`.\n4. `/speckit:save` is ambiguous: check that the `command-speckit-save` row in `runtime/lib/scorer/projection.ts` has `ownerSkill: 'system-spec-kit'`, then run `runtime/tests/scorer/same-owner-ambiguity.vitest.ts`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. SOURCE FILES\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Mode-to-command table, the effort word list and the single-mode rule |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` | Adds `command` beside `compiledRoute` and skips a command whose own row already ranks |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/ambiguity.ts` | Keeps a command row and its owner out of the same tie |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` | Unit tests for the table and the effort gate |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/scorer/same-owner-ambiguity.vitest.ts` | Unit tests for the same-owner tie rule |\n| [`advisor-recommend.md`](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md) | Feature catalog entry for `advisor_recommend` |\n| [`ambiguity.md`](../../feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/ambiguity.md) | Feature catalog entry for the ambiguity window |\n\n---\n\n## 5. SOURCE METADATA\n\n- Group: Native Command Surface\n- Playbook ID: NC-011\n- Canonical root source: [manual-testing-playbook.md](../manual-testing-playbook.md)\n- Feature file path: native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"     360 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md\n     124 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md\n     484 total\n=== hub chapter ===\n---\ntitle: \"NC-011 Hub Recommendation Names Its Slash Command\"\ndescription: \"Manual validation that advisor_recommend adds the routed mode's slash command to a hub recommendation, holds a deep-loop command back until the prompt asks for depth and does not report a command row and its owner as a tie.\"\nversion: 1.0.0.0\nid: NC-011\ncategory: native_cli_tools\nstage: routing\n---\n\n# NC-011 Hub Recommendation Names Its Slash Command\n\nPrompt: Manual validation that advisor_recommend adds the routed mode's slash command to a hub recommendation, holds a deep-loop command back until the prompt asks for depth and does not report a command row and its owner as a tie.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nA hub skill such as `sk-doc` used to come back as a bare skill id. The reader then had to work out which slash command runs the mode the hub picked. When a hub's compiled route picks exactly one mode, the recommendation now carries a `command` field with that mode's slash command.\n\nThis scenario checks three rules with four real prompts:\n\n1. A plain authoring prompt for `sk-doc` returns `command: \"/create:readme\"`.\n2. A `system-deep-loop` command is attached only when the prompt asks for depth. A prompt that names the topic gets the same route and no command.\n3. A command row and the skill that owns it are one answer, so their near tie does not set `ambiguous`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n\n- Objective: Confirm the `command` field on a single-mode hub route, the deep-loop effort gate and the same-owner tie rule.\n- Real user request: `When the advisor picks sk-doc for my README, does it tell me which command to type?`\n- Prompt: `Run advisor_recommend on four prompts and check which recommendations carry a slash command and whether any result is marked ambiguous.`\n- Expected execution process: Run the four commands below from the repository root and read the fields named under Expected.\n- Expected signals: `/create:readme` on `sk-doc`, no `command` key on the topic-only deep-loop prompt, `/deep:research` on the same prompt with `in depth` added, and `ambiguous: false` on `/speckit:save`.\n- Desired user-visible outcome: The reader learns which command to type for a hub's mode, and is never offered a long deep-loop run they did not ask for.\n- Pass/fail: PASS if all four results match the expected signals. FAIL if any command is missing, extra or wrong, or if `/speckit:save` is marked ambiguous.\n\n---\n\n## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n\n### Prompt\n\n- Prompt: `Run advisor_recommend on four prompts and check which recommendations carry a slash command and whether any result is marked ambiguous.`\n\n### Commands\n\n1. `bash: mkdir -p /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook`\n2. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"create a readme for the scripts folder\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-011-readme.json`\n3. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"research loop on incremental indexing approaches\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-011-topic.json`\n4. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"research loop on incremental indexing approaches in depth\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-011-depth.json`\n5. `bash: node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"/speckit:save\"}' --format json > /tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/nc-011-owner.json`\n6. In each file read `status`, `data.ambiguous` and, for each entry in `data.recommendations`, the `skillId`, `score`, `confidence`, `compiledRoute.targets[0].workflowMode` and `command`.\n\n### Exact Prompt Table\n\n| Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| NC-011 | Hub recommendation names its slash command | Confirm the `command` field, the deep-loop effort gate and the same-owner tie rule | `Run advisor_recommend on four prompts and check which recommendations carry a slash command and whether any result is marked ambiguous.` | Commands 1 to 6 above | `/create:readme` on `sk-doc`. No `command` on the topic prompt. `/deep:research` on the depth prompt. `ambiguous: false` on `/speckit:save` | The four JSON files plus the terminal transcript | PASS if all four results match. FAIL otherwise | See Failure Triage |\n\n### Expected\n\n- Every envelope has `status: \"ok\"` and the shell exit code is `0`.\n- `nc-011-readme.json`: the first recommendation is `sk-doc`. Its `compiledRoute` has `action: \"route\"`, `selectionKind: \"single\"` and one target with `workflowMode: \"sk-create-readme\"`. It carries `command: \"/create:readme\"`.\n- `nc-011-topic.json`: the first recommendation is `system-deep-loop` with a single route to the `research` mode. The recommendation has no `command` key, because the prompt names the topic and asks for no depth.\n- `nc-011-depth.json`: the same skill and the same `research` route, now with `command: \"/deep:research\"`. The words `in depth` are on the effort list, so they unlock the command.\n- `nc-011-owner.json`: `system-spec-kit` and `command-speckit-save` both rank, with scores less than 0.05 apart. `data.ambiguous` is `false`, because `command-speckit-save` belongs to `system-spec-kit`.\n\n### Evidence\n\nKeep the four JSON files and the terminal transcript. Reduced to one line per recommendation, a passing run reads like this:\n\n```text\ncreate a readme for the scripts folder\n  status=ok ambiguous=false\n  sk-doc score=0.78436 confidence=0.9416 route=route/single/sk-create-readme command=/create:readme\nresearch loop on incremental indexing approaches\n  status=ok ambiguous=false\n  system-deep-loop score=0.675806 confidence=0.8832 route=route/single/research command=(absent)\nresearch loop on incremental indexing approaches in depth\n  status=ok ambiguous=false\n  system-deep-loop score=0.65573 confidence=0.8725 route=route/single/research command=/deep:research\n/speckit:save\n  status=ok ambiguous=false\n  system-spec-kit score=0.738163 confidence=0.9168 command=(absent)\n  command-speckit-save score=0.7 confidence=0.8962 command=(absent)\n```\n\nThe scores can move after an index rebuild. The commands, the routes and the `ambiguous` value are the checked signals.\n\n### Pass / Fail\n\n- **Pass**: all four results match Expected.\n- **Fail**: `sk-doc` has no `command` or a different one, the topic prompt carries a command, the depth prompt does not carry `/deep:research`, or `/speckit:save` returns `ambiguous: true`.\n\n### Failure Triage\n\n1. `sk-doc` has no `compiledRoute` at all: run `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs`. A `stale-manifest` line for `sk-doc` means the hub serves legacy routing until its manifest is refreshed, and legacy routing names no command.\n2. A route exists but no command: check that the routed mode has a row in `MODE_COMMANDS` in `runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, and that the runtime build is current.\n3. The topic prompt carries a deep-loop command: compare the prompt with `DEPTH_WORDING` in `runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`.\n4. `/speckit:save` is ambiguous: check that the `command-speckit-save` row in `runtime/lib/scorer/projection.ts` has `ownerSkill: 'system-spec-kit'`, then run `runtime/tests/scorer/same-owner-ambiguity.vitest.ts`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. SOURCE FILES\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts` | Mode-to-command table, the effort word list and the single-mode rule |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` | Adds `command` beside `compiledRoute` and skips a command whose own row already ranks |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/ambiguity.ts` | Keeps a command row and its owner out of the same tie |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` | Unit tests for the table and the effort gate |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/scorer/same-owner-ambiguity.vitest.ts` | Unit tests for the same-owner tie rule |\n| [`advisor-recommend.md`](../../feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md) | Feature catalog entry for `advisor_recommend` |\n| [`ambiguity.md`](../../feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/ambiguity.md) | Feature catalog entry for the ambiguity window |\n\n---\n\n## 5. SOURCE METADATA\n\n- Group: Native Command Surface\n- Playbook ID: NC-011\n- Canonical root source: [manual-testing-playbook.md](../manual-testing-playbook.md)\n- Feature file path: native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research; wc -l .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md; echo \\\"=== hub chapter ===\\\"; cat .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794570727406,"end":1794570728348}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794570729295,"sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","part":{"id":"prt_1d4c0374f001Brp4Az0lHO1FUP","sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","messageID":"msg_1d4c013d4001oJj62JjTHjlmlx","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"38fb4d760d656f6e10e5c1cdd63e32c077d9d2ee","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":522,"output":158,"reasoning":265,"cache":{"read":201216,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794570733692,"sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","part":{"id":"prt_1d4c0487c001QfsaGwHuJ54S4P","sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","messageID":"msg_1d4c037540011IWvCvDBkrwOAk","type":"step-start","snapshot":"38fb4d760d656f6e10e5c1cdd63e32c077d9d2ee"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794570736557,"sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4c052ae002D8ORAnRVMXUtGC","sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","messageID":"msg_1d4c037540011IWvCvDBkrwOAk","type":"tool","id":"call_b92a60a44d8c4deca970f1d7","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md, lines 1-360\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Skill Advisor: Manual Testing Playbook\"\n3: description: \"Canonical sk-doc manual testing playbook for the Skill Advisor manual scenario corpus across the native command surface, runtime hooks, compatibility, daemon state, indexing, lifecycle, scorer and Python compatibility workflows.\"\n4: version: 0.8.10.0\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Skill Advisor: Manual Testing Playbook\n8: \n9: This document combines the canonical manual-validation contract for the `skill_advisor` package into a single reference. The root playbook acts as the operator directory, review protocol and orchestration guide while the per-feature files carry scenario-specific execution truth.\n10: \n11: ---\n12: \n13: This playbook package follows the sk-doc manual testing playbook template. The current corpus is the historical operator scenario set merged into the canonical manual-testing package on 2026-05-07, with former `SAD-NNN` scenarios absorbed into their current `NC` and `CL` homes.\n14: \n15: Canonical package artifacts:\n16: - `manual-testing-playbook.md`\n17: - `native-cli-tools/`\n18: - `cli-hooks-and-plugin/`\n19: - `compat-and-disable/`\n20: - `operator-h5/`\n21: - `auto-update-daemon/`\n22: - `auto-indexing/`\n23: - `lifecycle-routing/`\n24: - `scorer-fusion/`\n25: - `python-compat/`\n26: \n27: ### Result persistence\n28: \n29: <!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n30: A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `system-skill-advisor/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n35: \n36: This playbook provides 50 scenario files across 9 categories, one of them a live conversation scenario, validating the Skill Advisor surface. Scenario IDs use a multi-prefix scheme: `NC` for the native command surface, `CL` for CLI hooks plus plugin behavior, `CP` for compatibility plus disable controls, `OP` for operator H5 states, `AU` for auto-update daemon behavior, `AI` for auto-indexing, `LC` for lifecycle routing, `SC` for scorer fusion and `PC` for Python compatibility.\n37: \n38: > **Numbering note (gap-09).** The directory layout skips slot `09--*` between `scorer-fusion` and `python-compat`. This mirrors the `feature-catalog/` 05-gap pattern and is an intentional historical reservation from initial scaffold design. The gap is preserved to keep spec-folder cross-reference stability across packets. Do not renumber.\n39: \n40: Coverage note (2026-05-07): the prior operator scenario corpus now lives under `manual-testing-playbook/` as the canonical manual validation package. Former `SAD-NNN` IDs are deprecated plus mapped to current feature files in section 18. The source root index describes 42 scenarios, but the source category tree contains 41 `NNN-*.md` scenario files plus no `CL-002`. This root playbook reflects the live file inventory.\n41: \n42: ### Realistic Test Model\n43: \n44: 1. A realistic user request is given to an operator or runtime.\n45: 2. The operator decides whether to call the native command through the CLI, run a prompt-time hook, execute compatibility shims or inspect daemon/indexing behavior.\n46: 3. The operator captures command transcripts, CLI JSON envelopes, hook stdout/stderr and final verdicts.\n47: 4. The scenario passes only when observed output is prompt-safe, user-visible and consistent with the documented feature contract.\n48: \n49: ### What Each Feature File Should Explain\n50: \n51: - The realistic user request or operational state that should trigger the behavior.\n52: - The deterministic setup and execution steps.\n53: - The expected signals and failure modes.\n54: - The desired user-visible outcome.\n55: - The implementation, automated test or catalog anchors that justify the scenario.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. GLOBAL PRECONDITIONS\n60: \n61: 1. Working directory is the repository root.\n62: 2. Node.js and Python 3 are available.\n63: 3. The advisor runtime build is current:\n64: \n65: ```bash\n66: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n67: ```\n68: \n69: 4. `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` is unset unless a scenario explicitly sets it.\n70: 5. Operators can run the documented advisor CLI and Node/Python commands from the repo root.\n71: 6. Destructive or stateful scenarios use a disposable workspace copy unless the scenario explicitly validates the live checkout.\n72: \n73: ---\n74: \n75: ## 3. GLOBAL EVIDENCE REQUIREMENTS\n76: \n77: - Scenario ID and per-feature file path.\n78: - User request or operational condition used.\n79: - Exact command transcript or CLI call payload.\n80: - Full JSON output or a focused excerpt containing asserted fields.\n81: - Exit code for shell commands.\n82: - Hook stdout and stderr captured separately when hook scripts are involved.\n83: - Final user-facing verdict with rationale.\n84: - Triage notes for every `FAIL`, `PARTIAL` or `SKIP`.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. DETERMINISTIC COMMAND NOTATION\n89: \n90: - CLI commands shown as `bash: <command>`.\n91: - CLI calls shown as `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs <command> --format json`.\n92: - Hook stdin payloads shown as JSON piped into the compiled hook script.\n93: - `->` separates sequential steps inside one scenario contract.\n94: - File references use repo-root-relative paths unless otherwise stated.\n95: \n96: ---\n97: \n98: ## 5. REVIEW PROTOCOL AND RELEASE READINESS\n99: \n100: ### Inputs Required\n101: \n102: 1. `manual-testing-playbook.md`.\n103: 2. Referenced per-feature files under `manual-testing-playbook/NN__category_name/`.\n104: 3. Scenario execution evidence.\n105: 4. Feature-to-scenario coverage map.\n106: 5. Triage notes for all non-pass outcomes.\n107: \n108: ### Scenario Acceptance Rules\n109: \n110: For each executed scenario, check:\n111: \n112: 1. Preconditions were satisfied.\n113: 2. Prompt and command sequence were executed as written.\n114: 3. Expected signals are present in the captured output.\n115: 4. Evidence is complete and readable.\n116: 5. Raw prompt text is not leaked into diagnostics, attribution, cache metadata or trust-state fields unless the scenario explicitly validates input echo behavior.\n117: 6. Outcome rationale is explicit and references the user-visible behavior.\n118: \n119: Scenario verdict:\n120: - `PASS`: all acceptance checks true.\n121: - `PARTIAL`: core behavior works but non-critical evidence or metadata is incomplete.\n122: - `FAIL`: expected behavior missing, contradictory output appears, prompt text leaks or a critical check failed.\n123: - `SKIP`: a real sandbox, dependency or runtime blocker prevents execution and is documented.\n124: \n125: ### Feature Verdict Rules\n126: \n127: - `PASS`: all mapped scenarios for the feature are `PASS`.\n128: - `PARTIAL`: at least one mapped scenario is `PARTIAL`, none are `FAIL`.\n129: - `FAIL`: any mapped scenario is `FAIL`.\n130: \n131: ### Release Readiness Rule\n132: \n133: Release is `READY` only when all 50 scenario files are `PASS` or have an approved `SKIP` with a real blocker and no prompt-safety, rebuild, daemon, indexing, lifecycle, scorer or compatibility failure remains unresolved.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 6. SUB-AGENT ORCHESTRATION AND WAVE PLANNING\n138: \n139: ### Purpose\n140: \n141: This section records wave planning for the canonical Skill Advisor manual test package. The scenario corpus is large enough to split across operators while keeping category ownership clear.\n142: \n143: ### Operational Rules\n144: \n145: 1. Probe build state first with the advisor runtime build command.\n146: 2. Run native command-surface scenarios before hook scenarios so runtime failures can be separated from scorer or command failures.\n147: 3. Run rebuild and daemon scenarios in disposable workspaces when validating repair or generated state.\n148: 4. Assign explicit scenario IDs before parallel execution.\n149: 5. Keep stdout, stderr and CLI JSON evidence in scenario-specific files under `/tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/`.\n150: 6. After each wave, record verdicts and blockers before starting the next wave.\n151: \n152: ### Recommended Wave Plan\n153: \n154: - **Wave 1**: `NC-001..NC-011` native command-surface behavior.\n155: - **Wave 2**: `CL-001`, `CL-004..CL-008` runtime hooks, the OpenCode plugin, CLI fallback and the goal plugin.\n156: - **Wave 3**: `CP-001..CP-004` compatibility and disable controls.\n157: - **Wave 4**: `OP-001..OP-003` H5 operator states.\n158: - **Wave 5**: `AU-001..AU-005` auto-update daemon behavior.\n159: - **Wave 6**: `AI-001..AI-007` auto-indexing behavior.\n160: - **Wave 7**: `LC-001..LC-005` lifecycle routing.\n161: - **Wave 8**: `SC-001..SC-005` scorer fusion.\n162: - **Wave 9**: `PC-001..PC-005` Python compatibility.\n163: \n164: ---\n165: \n166: ## 7. NATIVE COMMAND SURFACE\n167: \n168: This category validates native command-surface scenarios `NC-001..NC-011`.\n169: \n170: | ID | Scenario | File |\n171: |---|---|---|\n172: | NC-001 | Native advisor_recommend Happy Path | [native-recommend-happy-path.md](native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md) |\n173: | NC-002 | Native advisor_status Transitions | [native-status-transitions.md](native-cli-tools/native-status-transitions.md) |\n174: | NC-003 | Native advisor_validate Slice Bundle | [native-validate-slices.md](native-cli-tools/native-validate-slices.md) |\n175: | NC-004 | Ambiguous Brief Rendering | [ambiguous-brief-rendering.md](native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md) |\n176: | NC-005 | Lifecycle Redirect Metadata | [lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md](native-cli-tools/lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md) |\n177: | NC-006 | Advisor Status and Rebuild Separation | [advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md](native-cli-tools/advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md) |\n178: | NC-007 | Skill Graph Status | [skill-graph-status.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-status.md) |\n179: | NC-008 | Skill Graph Query | [skill-graph-query.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-query.md) |\n180: | NC-009 | Skill Graph Validate | [skill-graph-validate.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-validate.md) |\n181: | NC-010 | Shadow-Delta Sink Opt-In | [shadow-delta-sink.md](native-cli-tools/shadow-delta-sink.md) |\n182: | NC-011 | Hub Recommendation Names Its Slash Command | [hub-command-attachment.md](native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md) |\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 8. CLI HOOKS AND PLUGIN\n187: \n188: This category validates cli hooks and plugin scenarios `CL-001`, `CL-004..CL-008`.\n189: \n190: | ID | Scenario | File |\n191: |---|---|---|\n192: | CL-001 | Claude Code UserPromptSubmit Hook | [001-claude-user-prompt-submit.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/claude-user-prompt-submit.md) |\n193: | CL-004 | OpenCode Native Hooks And Wrapper Fallback | 004-opencode-hook-and-wrapper.md (not yet authored) |\n194: | CL-005 | OpenCode Plugin | [005-opencode-plugin-bridge.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/opencode-plugin-bridge.md) |\n195: | CL-006 | skill-advisor CLI Fallback Surface (028) | [006-skill-advisor-cli-fallback.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.md) |\n196: | CL-007 | Goal OpenCode Plugin | [goal-opencode-plugin.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin.md) |\n197: | CL-008 | Goal OpenCode Later-Turn Injection | [goal-opencode-plugin-conversation.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin-conversation.md) |\n198: \n199: ---\n200: \n201: ## 9. COMPAT AND DISABLE\n202: \n203: This category validates compat and disable scenarios `CP-001..CP-004`.\n204: \n205: | ID | Scenario | File |\n206: |---|---|---|\n207: | CP-001 | Python Shim --stdin Mode | [001-python-shim-stdin.md](compat-and-disable/python-shim-stdin.md) |\n208: | CP-002 | Force Local And Force Native Toggles | [002-force-local-force-native.md](compat-and-disable/force-local-force-native.md) |\n209: | CP-003 | Global Disable Flag | [003-global-disable-flag.md](compat-and-disable/global-disable-flag.md) |\n210: | CP-004 | Daemon Absent Fallback | [004-daemon-absent-fallback.md](compat-and-disable/daemon-absent-fallback.md) |\n211: \n212: ---\n213: \n214: ## 10. OPERATOR H5\n215: \n216: This category validates operator h5 scenarios `OP-001..OP-003`.\n217: \n218: | ID | Scenario | File |\n219: |---|---|---|\n220: | OP-001 | Degraded Daemon | [001-degraded-daemon.md](operator-h5/degraded-daemon.md) |\n221: | OP-002 | Quarantined Daemon | [002-quarantined-daemon.md](operator-h5/quarantined-daemon.md) |\n222: | OP-003 | Unavailable Daemon | [003-unavailable-daemon.md](operator-h5/unavailable-daemon.md) |\n223: \n224: ---\n225: \n226: ## 11. AUTO UPDATE DAEMON\n227: \n228: This category validates auto update daemon scenarios `AU-001..AU-005`.\n229: \n230: | ID | Scenario | File |\n231: |---|---|---|\n232: | AU-001 | Chokidar Watcher Narrow Scope | [001-watcher-narrow-scope.md](auto-update-daemon/watcher-narrow-scope.md) |\n233: | AU-002 | Workspace Single-Writer Lease | [002-lease-single-writer.md](auto-update-daemon/lease-single-writer.md) |\n234: | AU-003 | Daemon Lifecycle and SIGTERM | [003-daemon-lifecycle-shutdown.md](auto-update-daemon/daemon-lifecycle-shutdown.md) |\n235: | AU-004 | Generation-Tagged Snapshot Publication | [004-generation-publication.md](auto-update-daemon/generation-publication.md) |\n236: | AU-005 | Rebuild From Source on Corrupt SQLite | [005-rebuild-from-source.md](auto-update-daemon/rebuild-from-source.md) |\n237: \n238: ---\n239: \n240: ## 12. AUTO INDEXING\n241: \n242: This category validates auto indexing scenarios `AI-001..AI-007`.\n243: \n244: | ID | Scenario | File |\n245: |---|---|---|\n246: | AI-001 | Deterministic Derived Extraction | [001-derived-extraction.md](auto-indexing/derived-extraction.md) |\n247: | AI-002 | A7 Sanitizer at Every Write Boundary | [002-sanitizer-boundaries.md](auto-indexing/sanitizer-boundaries.md) |\n248: | AI-003 | Provenance Fingerprints and Trust Lanes | [003-provenance-and-trust-lanes.md](auto-indexing/provenance-and-trust-lanes.md) |\n249: | AI-004 | DF/IDF Corpus Stats Active-Only | [004-corpus-df-idf.md](auto-indexing/corpus-df-idf.md) |\n250: | AI-005 | Anti-Stuffing and Cardinality Caps | [005-anti-stuffing.md](auto-indexing/anti-stuffing.md) |\n251: | AI-006 | Doc-Frontmatter Trigger Harvest | [006-doc-frontmatter-harvest.md](auto-indexing/doc-frontmatter-harvest.md) |\n252: | AI-007 | Private Skill Roots Variable | [private-skill-roots.md](auto-indexing/private-skill-roots.md) |\n253: \n254: ---\n255: \n256: ## 13. LIFECYCLE ROUTING\n257: \n258: This category validates lifecycle routing scenarios `LC-001..LC-005`.\n259: \n260: | ID | Scenario | File |\n261: |---|---|---|\n262: | LC-001 | Derived-Lane-Only Age Haircut | [001-age-haircut.md](lifecycle-routing/age-haircut.md) |\n263: | LC-002 | Asymmetric Supersession Redirects | [002-supersession.md](lifecycle-routing/supersession.md) |\n264: | LC-003 | Archive and Future Skills Indexed But Not Routed | [003-archive-handling.md](lifecycle-routing/archive-handling.md) |\n265: | LC-004 | Schema v1 to v2 Additive Backfill | [004-schema-migration.md](lifecycle-routing/schema-migration.md) |\n266: | LC-005 | Lifecycle-Level Rollback | [005-rollback-lifecycle.md](lifecycle-routing/rollback-lifecycle.md) |\n267: \n268: ---\n269: \n270: ## 14. SCORER FUSION\n271: \n272: This category validates scorer fusion scenarios `SC-001..SC-005`.\n273: \n274: | ID | Scenario | File |\n275: |---|---|---|\n276: | SC-001 | Five-Lane Analytical Fusion | [001-five-lane-fusion.md](scorer-fusion/five-lane-fusion.md) |\n277: | SC-002 | Registry Projection Drift Guard and workflowMode Publication | [002-projection.md](scorer-fusion/projection.md) |\n278: | SC-003 | Top-2 Ambiguity Window | [003-ambiguity.md](scorer-fusion/ambiguity.md) |\n279: | SC-004 | Lane Contribution Attribution | [004-lane-attribution.md](scorer-fusion/lane-attribution.md) |\n280: | SC-005 | Lane-by-Lane Ablation Protocol | [005-ablation.md](scorer-fusion/ablation.md) |\n281: \n282: ---\n283: \n284: ## 15. PYTHON COMPAT\n285: \n286: This category validates python compat scenarios `PC-001..PC-005`.\n287: \n288: | ID | Scenario | File |\n289: |---|---|---|\n290: | PC-001 | Python Shim --stdin Round-Trip | [001-stdin-mode.md](python-compat/stdin-mode.md) |\n291: | PC-002 | --force-native and --force-local Toggles | [002-force-native-force-local.md](python-compat/force-native-force-local.md) |\n292: | PC-003 | threshold Confidence Flag | [003-threshold-flag.md](python-compat/threshold-flag.md) |\n293: | PC-004 | Python Regression Dataset | [004-regression-suite.md](python-compat/regression-suite.md) |\n294: | PC-005 | Python Bench Runner | [005-bench-runner.md](python-compat/bench-runner.md) |\n295: \n296: ---\n297: \n298: ## 16. DOCTOR COMMAND SCENARIOS\n299: \n300: Doctor command scenarios for the Skill Advisor now live in the [central manual testing playbook](../../../manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md).\n301: \n302: ---\n303: \n304: ## 17. AUTOMATED TEST CROSS-REFERENCE\n305: \n306: The active inventory check lives at `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/manual-testing-playbook.vitest.ts`. It verifies the root playbook rows, the live per-feature file inventory and the scenario package count.\n307: \n308: | Area | Automated test anchors |\n309: |---|---|\n310: | Native command surface | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/advisor-rebuild.vitest.ts` |\n311: | Hooks and plugin | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/settings-driven-invocation-parity.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/runtime-parity.vitest.ts`, `.opencode/plugins/tests/opencode-goal-state.test.cjs`, `.opencode/plugins/tests/opencode-goal-continuation.test.cjs` |\n312: | Compatibility and Python entrypoints | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/manual-testing-playbook.vitest.ts` |\n313: | Scoring, lifecycle, indexing and daemon state | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` |\n314: \n315: Validator limitation: `validate_document.py` validates this root document and per-feature documents individually, but it does not prove that every command can execute in the current sandbox. Operators must capture execution evidence during release review.\n316: \n317: ---\n318: \n319: ## 18. FEATURE CATALOG CROSS-REFERENCE INDEX\n320: \n321: | Scenario prefix | Category | Feature file directory |\n322: |---|---|---|\n323: | NC | Native command surface | `native-cli-tools/` |\n324: | CL | CLI hooks and plugin | `cli-hooks-and-plugin/` |\n325: | CP | Compat and disable | `compat-and-disable/` |\n326: | OP | Operator H5 | `operator-h5/` |\n327: | AU | Auto update daemon | `auto-update-daemon/` |\n328: | AI | Auto indexing | `auto-indexing/` |\n329: | LC | Lifecycle routing | `lifecycle-routing/` |\n330: | SC | Scorer fusion | `scorer-fusion/` |\n331: | PC | Python compat | `python-compat/` |\n332: \n333: Per-feature files include SOURCE FILES sections with implementation, catalog and test anchors where the source scenario provided them.\n334: \n335: ### 18.5 Catalog group ↔ playbook category mapping (F37 cross-reference)\n336: \n337: The catalog uses 7 groups. The playbook uses 9 categories. Mapping is intentionally asymmetric, not 1:1. This table documents the relationships so operators can navigate between surfaces without surprise.\n338: \n339: | Feature catalog group | Maps to playbook category | Notes |\n340: |---|---|---|\n341: | `daemon-and-freshness` | `auto-update-daemon` (primary) plus `operator-h5` (operator state subset) | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: daemon mechanics in 05, operator-facing trust-state scenarios in 04 |\n342: | `auto-indexing` | `auto-indexing` | 1:1 mapping |\n343: | `lifecycle-routing` | `lifecycle-routing` | 1:1 mapping |\n344: | `scorer-fusion` | `scorer-fusion` | 1:1 mapping |\n345: | `cli-surface` | `native-cli-tools` plus `cli-hooks-and-plugin` | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: native command-surface scenarios in 01, CLI hook plus plugin invocation scenarios in 02 |\n346: | `hooks-and-plugin` | `cli-hooks-and-plugin` (primary) plus `compat-and-disable` (rollback states) | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: happy-path hook behavior in 02, disable plus rollback flags in 03 |\n347: | `python-compat` | `python-compat` | 1:1 mapping (note gap-09 is intentional per playbook §1) |\n348: | (none) | `compat-and-disable` | Compat plus disable flags cut across multiple catalog groups. No single catalog group owns them |\n349: | (none) | `operator-h5` | H5 operator state scenarios cut across catalog groups. No single catalog group owns them |\n350: \n351: **Why asymmetric**: catalog groups model feature ownership (what the code does), while playbook categories model operator workflow (what an operator validates in one sitting). A single feature group can require multiple operator scenarios that span different runtime contexts (native command surface vs CLI hook vs compat shim). The asymmetry is documented here rather than removed by renumbering because renumbering would break checked-in inventory tests plus deep cross-references in earlier packets.\n352: \n353: ---\n354: \n355: ## 19. LEGACY ID CROSS-REFERENCE\n356: \n357: - SAD-001 -> NC-001 (native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md)\n358: - SAD-002 -> NC-004 (native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md)\n359: - SAD-003 -> CL-001 (cli-hooks-and-plugin/claude-user-prompt-submit.md)\n360: - SAD-004 -> NC-006 (native-cli-tools/advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md, lines 1-360\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Skill Advisor: Manual Testing Playbook\"\n3: description: \"Canonical sk-doc manual testing playbook for the Skill Advisor manual scenario corpus across the native command surface, runtime hooks, compatibility, daemon state, indexing, lifecycle, scorer and Python compatibility workflows.\"\n4: version: 0.8.10.0\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Skill Advisor: Manual Testing Playbook\n8: \n9: This document combines the canonical manual-validation contract for the `skill_advisor` package into a single reference. The root playbook acts as the operator directory, review protocol and orchestration guide while the per-feature files carry scenario-specific execution truth.\n10: \n11: ---\n12: \n13: This playbook package follows the sk-doc manual testing playbook template. The current corpus is the historical operator scenario set merged into the canonical manual-testing package on 2026-05-07, with former `SAD-NNN` scenarios absorbed into their current `NC` and `CL` homes.\n14: \n15: Canonical package artifacts:\n16: - `manual-testing-playbook.md`\n17: - `native-cli-tools/`\n18: - `cli-hooks-and-plugin/`\n19: - `compat-and-disable/`\n20: - `operator-h5/`\n21: - `auto-update-daemon/`\n22: - `auto-indexing/`\n23: - `lifecycle-routing/`\n24: - `scorer-fusion/`\n25: - `python-compat/`\n26: \n27: ### Result persistence\n28: \n29: <!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n30: A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `system-skill-advisor/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n35: \n36: This playbook provides 50 scenario files across 9 categories, one of them a live conversation scenario, validating the Skill Advisor surface. Scenario IDs use a multi-prefix scheme: `NC` for the native command surface, `CL` for CLI hooks plus plugin behavior, `CP` for compatibility plus disable controls, `OP` for operator H5 states, `AU` for auto-update daemon behavior, `AI` for auto-indexing, `LC` for lifecycle routing, `SC` for scorer fusion and `PC` for Python compatibility.\n37: \n38: > **Numbering note (gap-09).** The directory layout skips slot `09--*` between `scorer-fusion` and `python-compat`. This mirrors the `feature-catalog/` 05-gap pattern and is an intentional historical reservation from initial scaffold design. The gap is preserved to keep spec-folder cross-reference stability across packets. Do not renumber.\n39: \n40: Coverage note (2026-05-07): the prior operator scenario corpus now lives under `manual-testing-playbook/` as the canonical manual validation package. Former `SAD-NNN` IDs are deprecated plus mapped to current feature files in section 18. The source root index describes 42 scenarios, but the source category tree contains 41 `NNN-*.md` scenario files plus no `CL-002`. This root playbook reflects the live file inventory.\n41: \n42: ### Realistic Test Model\n43: \n44: 1. A realistic user request is given to an operator or runtime.\n45: 2. The operator decides whether to call the native command through the CLI, run a prompt-time hook, execute compatibility shims or inspect daemon/indexing behavior.\n46: 3. The operator captures command transcripts, CLI JSON envelopes, hook stdout/stderr and final verdicts.\n47: 4. The scenario passes only when observed output is prompt-safe, user-visible and consistent with the documented feature contract.\n48: \n49: ### What Each Feature File Should Explain\n50: \n51: - The realistic user request or operational state that should trigger the behavior.\n52: - The deterministic setup and execution steps.\n53: - The expected signals and failure modes.\n54: - The desired user-visible outcome.\n55: - The implementation, automated test or catalog anchors that justify the scenario.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. GLOBAL PRECONDITIONS\n60: \n61: 1. Working directory is the repository root.\n62: 2. Node.js and Python 3 are available.\n63: 3. The advisor runtime build is current:\n64: \n65: ```bash\n66: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n67: ```\n68: \n69: 4. `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` is unset unless a scenario explicitly sets it.\n70: 5. Operators can run the documented advisor CLI and Node/Python commands from the repo root.\n71: 6. Destructive or stateful scenarios use a disposable workspace copy unless the scenario explicitly validates the live checkout.\n72: \n73: ---\n74: \n75: ## 3. GLOBAL EVIDENCE REQUIREMENTS\n76: \n77: - Scenario ID and per-feature file path.\n78: - User request or operational condition used.\n79: - Exact command transcript or CLI call payload.\n80: - Full JSON output or a focused excerpt containing asserted fields.\n81: - Exit code for shell commands.\n82: - Hook stdout and stderr captured separately when hook scripts are involved.\n83: - Final user-facing verdict with rationale.\n84: - Triage notes for every `FAIL`, `PARTIAL` or `SKIP`.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. DETERMINISTIC COMMAND NOTATION\n89: \n90: - CLI commands shown as `bash: <command>`.\n91: - CLI calls shown as `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs <command> --format json`.\n92: - Hook stdin payloads shown as JSON piped into the compiled hook script.\n93: - `->` separates sequential steps inside one scenario contract.\n94: - File references use repo-root-relative paths unless otherwise stated.\n95: \n96: ---\n97: \n98: ## 5. REVIEW PROTOCOL AND RELEASE READINESS\n99: \n100: ### Inputs Required\n101: \n102: 1. `manual-testing-playbook.md`.\n103: 2. Referenced per-feature files under `manual-testing-playbook/NN__category_name/`.\n104: 3. Scenario execution evidence.\n105: 4. Feature-to-scenario coverage map.\n106: 5. Triage notes for all non-pass outcomes.\n107: \n108: ### Scenario Acceptance Rules\n109: \n110: For each executed scenario, check:\n111: \n112: 1. Preconditions were satisfied.\n113: 2. Prompt and command sequence were executed as written.\n114: 3. Expected signals are present in the captured output.\n115: 4. Evidence is complete and readable.\n116: 5. Raw prompt text is not leaked into diagnostics, attribution, cache metadata or trust-state fields unless the scenario explicitly validates input echo behavior.\n117: 6. Outcome rationale is explicit and references the user-visible behavior.\n118: \n119: Scenario verdict:\n120: - `PASS`: all acceptance checks true.\n121: - `PARTIAL`: core behavior works but non-critical evidence or metadata is incomplete.\n122: - `FAIL`: expected behavior missing, contradictory output appears, prompt text leaks or a critical check failed.\n123: - `SKIP`: a real sandbox, dependency or runtime blocker prevents execution and is documented.\n124: \n125: ### Feature Verdict Rules\n126: \n127: - `PASS`: all mapped scenarios for the feature are `PASS`.\n128: - `PARTIAL`: at least one mapped scenario is `PARTIAL`, none are `FAIL`.\n129: - `FAIL`: any mapped scenario is `FAIL`.\n130: \n131: ### Release Readiness Rule\n132: \n133: Release is `READY` only when all 50 scenario files are `PASS` or have an approved `SKIP` with a real blocker and no prompt-safety, rebuild, daemon, indexing, lifecycle, scorer or compatibility failure remains unresolved.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 6. SUB-AGENT ORCHESTRATION AND WAVE PLANNING\n138: \n139: ### Purpose\n140: \n141: This section records wave planning for the canonical Skill Advisor manual test package. The scenario corpus is large enough to split across operators while keeping category ownership clear.\n142: \n143: ### Operational Rules\n144: \n145: 1. Probe build state first with the advisor runtime build command.\n146: 2. Run native command-surface scenarios before hook scenarios so runtime failures can be separated from scorer or command failures.\n147: 3. Run rebuild and daemon scenarios in disposable workspaces when validating repair or generated state.\n148: 4. Assign explicit scenario IDs before parallel execution.\n149: 5. Keep stdout, stderr and CLI JSON evidence in scenario-specific files under `/tmp/skill-advisor-playbook/`.\n150: 6. After each wave, record verdicts and blockers before starting the next wave.\n151: \n152: ### Recommended Wave Plan\n153: \n154: - **Wave 1**: `NC-001..NC-011` native command-surface behavior.\n155: - **Wave 2**: `CL-001`, `CL-004..CL-008` runtime hooks, the OpenCode plugin, CLI fallback and the goal plugin.\n156: - **Wave 3**: `CP-001..CP-004` compatibility and disable controls.\n157: - **Wave 4**: `OP-001..OP-003` H5 operator states.\n158: - **Wave 5**: `AU-001..AU-005` auto-update daemon behavior.\n159: - **Wave 6**: `AI-001..AI-007` auto-indexing behavior.\n160: - **Wave 7**: `LC-001..LC-005` lifecycle routing.\n161: - **Wave 8**: `SC-001..SC-005` scorer fusion.\n162: - **Wave 9**: `PC-001..PC-005` Python compatibility.\n163: \n164: ---\n165: \n166: ## 7. NATIVE COMMAND SURFACE\n167: \n168: This category validates native command-surface scenarios `NC-001..NC-011`.\n169: \n170: | ID | Scenario | File |\n171: |---|---|---|\n172: | NC-001 | Native advisor_recommend Happy Path | [native-recommend-happy-path.md](native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md) |\n173: | NC-002 | Native advisor_status Transitions | [native-status-transitions.md](native-cli-tools/native-status-transitions.md) |\n174: | NC-003 | Native advisor_validate Slice Bundle | [native-validate-slices.md](native-cli-tools/native-validate-slices.md) |\n175: | NC-004 | Ambiguous Brief Rendering | [ambiguous-brief-rendering.md](native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md) |\n176: | NC-005 | Lifecycle Redirect Metadata | [lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md](native-cli-tools/lifecycle-redirect-metadata.md) |\n177: | NC-006 | Advisor Status and Rebuild Separation | [advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md](native-cli-tools/advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md) |\n178: | NC-007 | Skill Graph Status | [skill-graph-status.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-status.md) |\n179: | NC-008 | Skill Graph Query | [skill-graph-query.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-query.md) |\n180: | NC-009 | Skill Graph Validate | [skill-graph-validate.md](native-cli-tools/skill-graph-validate.md) |\n181: | NC-010 | Shadow-Delta Sink Opt-In | [shadow-delta-sink.md](native-cli-tools/shadow-delta-sink.md) |\n182: | NC-011 | Hub Recommendation Names Its Slash Command | [hub-command-attachment.md](native-cli-tools/hub-command-attachment.md) |\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 8. CLI HOOKS AND PLUGIN\n187: \n188: This category validates cli hooks and plugin scenarios `CL-001`, `CL-004..CL-008`.\n189: \n190: | ID | Scenario | File |\n191: |---|---|---|\n192: | CL-001 | Claude Code UserPromptSubmit Hook | [001-claude-user-prompt-submit.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/claude-user-prompt-submit.md) |\n193: | CL-004 | OpenCode Native Hooks And Wrapper Fallback | 004-opencode-hook-and-wrapper.md (not yet authored) |\n194: | CL-005 | OpenCode Plugin | [005-opencode-plugin-bridge.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/opencode-plugin-bridge.md) |\n195: | CL-006 | skill-advisor CLI Fallback Surface (028) | [006-skill-advisor-cli-fallback.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.md) |\n196: | CL-007 | Goal OpenCode Plugin | [goal-opencode-plugin.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin.md) |\n197: | CL-008 | Goal OpenCode Later-Turn Injection | [goal-opencode-plugin-conversation.md](cli-hooks-and-plugin/goal-opencode-plugin-conversation.md) |\n198: \n199: ---\n200: \n201: ## 9. COMPAT AND DISABLE\n202: \n203: This category validates compat and disable scenarios `CP-001..CP-004`.\n204: \n205: | ID | Scenario | File |\n206: |---|---|---|\n207: | CP-001 | Python Shim --stdin Mode | [001-python-shim-stdin.md](compat-and-disable/python-shim-stdin.md) |\n208: | CP-002 | Force Local And Force Native Toggles | [002-force-local-force-native.md](compat-and-disable/force-local-force-native.md) |\n209: | CP-003 | Global Disable Flag | [003-global-disable-flag.md](compat-and-disable/global-disable-flag.md) |\n210: | CP-004 | Daemon Absent Fallback | [004-daemon-absent-fallback.md](compat-and-disable/daemon-absent-fallback.md) |\n211: \n212: ---\n213: \n214: ## 10. OPERATOR H5\n215: \n216: This category validates operator h5 scenarios `OP-001..OP-003`.\n217: \n218: | ID | Scenario | File |\n219: |---|---|---|\n220: | OP-001 | Degraded Daemon | [001-degraded-daemon.md](operator-h5/degraded-daemon.md) |\n221: | OP-002 | Quarantined Daemon | [002-quarantined-daemon.md](operator-h5/quarantined-daemon.md) |\n222: | OP-003 | Unavailable Daemon | [003-unavailable-daemon.md](operator-h5/unavailable-daemon.md) |\n223: \n224: ---\n225: \n226: ## 11. AUTO UPDATE DAEMON\n227: \n228: This category validates auto update daemon scenarios `AU-001..AU-005`.\n229: \n230: | ID | Scenario | File |\n231: |---|---|---|\n232: | AU-001 | Chokidar Watcher Narrow Scope | [001-watcher-narrow-scope.md](auto-update-daemon/watcher-narrow-scope.md) |\n233: | AU-002 | Workspace Single-Writer Lease | [002-lease-single-writer.md](auto-update-daemon/lease-single-writer.md) |\n234: | AU-003 | Daemon Lifecycle and SIGTERM | [003-daemon-lifecycle-shutdown.md](auto-update-daemon/daemon-lifecycle-shutdown.md) |\n235: | AU-004 | Generation-Tagged Snapshot Publication | [004-generation-publication.md](auto-update-daemon/generation-publication.md) |\n236: | AU-005 | Rebuild From Source on Corrupt SQLite | [005-rebuild-from-source.md](auto-update-daemon/rebuild-from-source.md) |\n237: \n238: ---\n239: \n240: ## 12. AUTO INDEXING\n241: \n242: This category validates auto indexing scenarios `AI-001..AI-007`.\n243: \n244: | ID | Scenario | File |\n245: |---|---|---|\n246: | AI-001 | Deterministic Derived Extraction | [001-derived-extraction.md](auto-indexing/derived-extraction.md) |\n247: | AI-002 | A7 Sanitizer at Every Write Boundary | [002-sanitizer-boundaries.md](auto-indexing/sanitizer-boundaries.md) |\n248: | AI-003 | Provenance Fingerprints and Trust Lanes | [003-provenance-and-trust-lanes.md](auto-indexing/provenance-and-trust-lanes.md) |\n249: | AI-004 | DF/IDF Corpus Stats Active-Only | [004-corpus-df-idf.md](auto-indexing/corpus-df-idf.md) |\n250: | AI-005 | Anti-Stuffing and Cardinality Caps | [005-anti-stuffing.md](auto-indexing/anti-stuffing.md) |\n251: | AI-006 | Doc-Frontmatter Trigger Harvest | [006-doc-frontmatter-harvest.md](auto-indexing/doc-frontmatter-harvest.md) |\n252: | AI-007 | Private Skill Roots Variable | [private-skill-roots.md](auto-indexing/private-skill-roots.md) |\n253: \n254: ---\n255: \n256: ## 13. LIFECYCLE ROUTING\n257: \n258: This category validates lifecycle routing scenarios `LC-001..LC-005`.\n259: \n260: | ID | Scenario | File |\n261: |---|---|---|\n262: | LC-001 | Derived-Lane-Only Age Haircut | [001-age-haircut.md](lifecycle-routing/age-haircut.md) |\n263: | LC-002 | Asymmetric Supersession Redirects | [002-supersession.md](lifecycle-routing/supersession.md) |\n264: | LC-003 | Archive and Future Skills Indexed But Not Routed | [003-archive-handling.md](lifecycle-routing/archive-handling.md) |\n265: | LC-004 | Schema v1 to v2 Additive Backfill | [004-schema-migration.md](lifecycle-routing/schema-migration.md) |\n266: | LC-005 | Lifecycle-Level Rollback | [005-rollback-lifecycle.md](lifecycle-routing/rollback-lifecycle.md) |\n267: \n268: ---\n269: \n270: ## 14. SCORER FUSION\n271: \n272: This category validates scorer fusion scenarios `SC-001..SC-005`.\n273: \n274: | ID | Scenario | File |\n275: |---|---|---|\n276: | SC-001 | Five-Lane Analytical Fusion | [001-five-lane-fusion.md](scorer-fusion/five-lane-fusion.md) |\n277: | SC-002 | Registry Projection Drift Guard and workflowMode Publication | [002-projection.md](scorer-fusion/projection.md) |\n278: | SC-003 | Top-2 Ambiguity Window | [003-ambiguity.md](scorer-fusion/ambiguity.md) |\n279: | SC-004 | Lane Contribution Attribution | [004-lane-attribution.md](scorer-fusion/lane-attribution.md) |\n280: | SC-005 | Lane-by-Lane Ablation Protocol | [005-ablation.md](scorer-fusion/ablation.md) |\n281: \n282: ---\n283: \n284: ## 15. PYTHON COMPAT\n285: \n286: This category validates python compat scenarios `PC-001..PC-005`.\n287: \n288: | ID | Scenario | File |\n289: |---|---|---|\n290: | PC-001 | Python Shim --stdin Round-Trip | [001-stdin-mode.md](python-compat/stdin-mode.md) |\n291: | PC-002 | --force-native and --force-local Toggles | [002-force-native-force-local.md](python-compat/force-native-force-local.md) |\n292: | PC-003 | threshold Confidence Flag | [003-threshold-flag.md](python-compat/threshold-flag.md) |\n293: | PC-004 | Python Regression Dataset | [004-regression-suite.md](python-compat/regression-suite.md) |\n294: | PC-005 | Python Bench Runner | [005-bench-runner.md](python-compat/bench-runner.md) |\n295: \n296: ---\n297: \n298: ## 16. DOCTOR COMMAND SCENARIOS\n299: \n300: Doctor command scenarios for the Skill Advisor now live in the [central manual testing playbook](../../../manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md).\n301: \n302: ---\n303: \n304: ## 17. AUTOMATED TEST CROSS-REFERENCE\n305: \n306: The active inventory check lives at `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/manual-testing-playbook.vitest.ts`. It verifies the root playbook rows, the live per-feature file inventory and the scenario package count.\n307: \n308: | Area | Automated test anchors |\n309: |---|---|\n310: | Native command surface | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/advisor-rebuild.vitest.ts` |\n311: | Hooks and plugin | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/settings-driven-invocation-parity.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/hooks/runtime-parity.vitest.ts`, `.opencode/plugins/tests/opencode-goal-state.test.cjs`, `.opencode/plugins/tests/opencode-goal-continuation.test.cjs` |\n312: | Compatibility and Python entrypoints | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/manual-testing-playbook.vitest.ts` |\n313: | Scoring, lifecycle, indexing and daemon state | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend.vitest.ts`, `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-privacy.vitest.ts` |\n314: \n315: Validator limitation: `validate_document.py` validates this root document and per-feature documents individually, but it does not prove that every command can execute in the current sandbox. Operators must capture execution evidence during release review.\n316: \n317: ---\n318: \n319: ## 18. FEATURE CATALOG CROSS-REFERENCE INDEX\n320: \n321: | Scenario prefix | Category | Feature file directory |\n322: |---|---|---|\n323: | NC | Native command surface | `native-cli-tools/` |\n324: | CL | CLI hooks and plugin | `cli-hooks-and-plugin/` |\n325: | CP | Compat and disable | `compat-and-disable/` |\n326: | OP | Operator H5 | `operator-h5/` |\n327: | AU | Auto update daemon | `auto-update-daemon/` |\n328: | AI | Auto indexing | `auto-indexing/` |\n329: | LC | Lifecycle routing | `lifecycle-routing/` |\n330: | SC | Scorer fusion | `scorer-fusion/` |\n331: | PC | Python compat | `python-compat/` |\n332: \n333: Per-feature files include SOURCE FILES sections with implementation, catalog and test anchors where the source scenario provided them.\n334: \n335: ### 18.5 Catalog group ↔ playbook category mapping (F37 cross-reference)\n336: \n337: The catalog uses 7 groups. The playbook uses 9 categories. Mapping is intentionally asymmetric, not 1:1. This table documents the relationships so operators can navigate between surfaces without surprise.\n338: \n339: | Feature catalog group | Maps to playbook category | Notes |\n340: |---|---|---|\n341: | `daemon-and-freshness` | `auto-update-daemon` (primary) plus `operator-h5` (operator state subset) | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: daemon mechanics in 05, operator-facing trust-state scenarios in 04 |\n342: | `auto-indexing` | `auto-indexing` | 1:1 mapping |\n343: | `lifecycle-routing` | `lifecycle-routing` | 1:1 mapping |\n344: | `scorer-fusion` | `scorer-fusion` | 1:1 mapping |\n345: | `cli-surface` | `native-cli-tools` plus `cli-hooks-and-plugin` | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: native command-surface scenarios in 01, CLI hook plus plugin invocation scenarios in 02 |\n346: | `hooks-and-plugin` | `cli-hooks-and-plugin` (primary) plus `compat-and-disable` (rollback states) | Catalog group split across 2 playbook categories: happy-path hook behavior in 02, disable plus rollback flags in 03 |\n347: | `python-compat` | `python-compat` | 1:1 mapping (note gap-09 is intentional per playbook §1) |\n348: | (none) | `compat-and-disable` | Compat plus disable flags cut across multiple catalog groups. No single catalog group owns them |\n349: | (none) | `operator-h5` | H5 operator state scenarios cut across catalog groups. No single catalog group owns them |\n350: \n351: **Why asymmetric**: catalog groups model feature ownership (what the code does), while playbook categories model operator workflow (what an operator validates in one sitting). A single feature group can require multiple operator scenarios that span different runtime contexts (native command surface vs CLI hook vs compat shim). The asymmetry is documented here rather than removed by renumbering because renumbering would break checked-in inventory tests plus deep cross-references in earlier packets.\n352: \n353: ---\n354: \n355: ## 19. LEGACY ID CROSS-REFERENCE\n356: \n357: - SAD-001 -> NC-001 (native-cli-tools/native-recommend-happy-path.md)\n358: - SAD-002 -> NC-004 (native-cli-tools/ambiguous-brief-rendering.md)\n359: - SAD-003 -> CL-001 (cli-hooks-and-plugin/claude-user-prompt-submit.md)\n360: - SAD-004 -> NC-006 (native-cli-tools/advisor-status-rebuild-separation.md)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794570736302,"end":1794570736557}}}}
