{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794572034633,"sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4d3c923002syc4jUMMqQPbig","sessionID":"ses_e2b4a2200ffebIrmmKNfkMdQAD","messageID":"msg_1d4d3a419001QqtLrpbLeRWPvn","type":"tool","id":"call_dbc84d95c105470fafbdd4fd","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Review Iteration 005\n\n## Dispatcher\n\n- Mode: review (fan-out lineage `accuracy-runner-2`, pass two)\n- Session: `fanout-accuracy-runner-2-1794570021136-noekp0`, generation 1, lineage mode `new`\n- Dimension: D4 Maintainability\n- Review target: `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build` (spec-folder)\n- Budget profile: verify (11 review actions)\n- Operator review focus bound as `review_focus`: pass-two review of command surfacing in the `accuracy-runner` area; look for new defects in the rest of the area, including the files not changed since pass one, and check that their contracts still describe what they do.\n- How this iteration addressed the focus: it read the restore helper end to end (a pass-one clean surface) and probed `git status --porcelain` quoting in a throwaway repository inside the lineage, then re-read the scorer's module header against the summary it actually prints. Two P2s: a restore path that cannot act on a quoted file name, and a header that promises an ordered read/write report the CLI never emits.\n- `steer.md`: absent before this iteration; no lead rulings to apply.\n\n## Files Reviewed\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/worktree-restore.mjs:1-122`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs:1140-1189`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1-43,1247-1280,1392-1427`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/worktree-restore.vitest.ts:1-122`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md:100-125`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-corpus-parity.vitest.ts:1-198`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-observability.vitest.ts` (health assertions only)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/measure-embedder-cost.mjs` (README claim only)\n\n## Findings - New\n\n### P0 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P1 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P2 Findings\n\n1. **A file name git quotes cannot be restored, so one space in a created path stops the arm and leaves the file** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/worktree-restore.mjs:54` -- `statusSnapshot` reads `git status --porcelain` and keys each path with `line.slice(3)`. Git quotes a path that contains a space (probe inside the lineage: `?? \"a b.txt\"`), so the map key is `\"a b.txt\"` including both quote characters. `restorePath` then asks `git cat-file -e 'HEAD:\"a b.txt\"'`, which is not a path git knows; `git rm --cached --ignore-unmatch -- '\"a b.txt\"'` also matches nothing; and `rmSync(resolve(cwd, '\"a b.txt\"'))` deletes a differently named file at most. The turn's file therefore stays, `leftoverPaths` reports the quoted key, and `restoreTurn` returns `restored: false`, so `runTurn` ends the arm with `restore_failed`. The failure is safe but unrecoverable by the runner: the next live arm refuses `worktree_dirty` on the leftover until someone deletes it by hand.\nFinding class: correctness / restore-completeness\nScope proof: the finding is inside the restore helper and its only caller; the probe wrote only inside the lineage's `logs/quotepath-probe` directory.\nAffected surface hints: `worktree-restore.mjs` `statusSnapshot` and `restorePath`; `run-command-behaviour.mjs` `runTurn`'s `restore_failed` branch; `tests/worktree-restore.vitest.ts`.\nCase: a measured turn writes `notes draft.md` in the arena root; `git status --porcelain` prints `?? \"notes draft.md\"`; after the turn the restore leaves the file, reports `restore_failed` with `paths: [\"\\\"notes draft.md\\\"\"]`, and the next arm refuses `worktree_dirty` until the operator removes it.\n\n2. **The transcript scorer's header promises an ordered read/write report the CLI does not print** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:5` -- The header says the tool \"reports what a model did with the commands a turn offered it: the `COMMAND:` lines it printed, the reads and writes in order, and whether a started loop ran its init first.\" The CLI has two outputs: `--summary` prints per-arm counts (`writeSummary`, line 1392-1427) and `--runtime --locator` prints a record count (`scoreSession`, line 885-901). The ordered read/write stream exists only inside `normalize` as an internal event list; no mode prints it. A maintainer looking for the ordered report the header names will search for an output path that does not exist, and may add a third mode instead of using `normalize` directly.\nFinding class: maintainability / documentation drift\nScope proof: the header and both output modes are in the declared area.\nAffected surface hints: `score-command-transcripts.mjs` module header and `writeSummary`; any future maintainer adding a per-session report.\nCase: a maintainer needs the write order for one session and reads the header; they expect the CLI to print it, find only counts and unscored reasons, and either add a redundant printer or read `normalize` from a test harness.\n\n## Traceability Checks\n\n- `spec_code`: pass. No new number claim was made this iteration; the five floors re-derived in iteration 4 still hold.\n- `checklist_evidence`: partial. No parent checklist; phase 007's open rows remain.\n- `skill_agent`: notApplicable.\n- `agent_cross_runtime`: partial. The restore defect is runtime-independent; the per-runtime proof stays phase 007's.\n- `feature_catalog_code`: pass (carried from iteration 4).\n- `playbook_capability`: partial (root playbook statements carry iteration 4's findings; NC-011 holds).\n\n## Integration Evidence\n\n- `worktree-restore.mjs` ↔ `run-command-behaviour.mjs`: `runTurn` treats `restored: false` as `restore_failed` and stops the arm, which is why the quoted-path defect surfaces as a blocked arm rather than a silent leftover.\n- `worktree-restore.mjs` ↔ `tests/worktree-restore.vitest.ts`: the test covers staged, deleted and inherited paths but no path with a space or a quote, which is why the defect survives a green suite.\n- `score-command-transcripts.mjs` ↔ `README.md:112`: the README describes the counts the CLI prints, while the module header describes a stream; the README is the accurate of the two.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- **The probe was local and minimal.** A throwaway `git init` inside the lineage's `logs/` folder showed the quoting for a space and a quote; it wrote nothing outside the lineage and was removed afterwards.\n- **The restore still fails safe.** The leftover is reported and the arm stops, so no wrong score is recorded; the cost is that a legitimate write blocks the next arm until a human intervenes.\n- **A renamed path is handled.** `statusSnapshot` splits on ` -> `, so both sides of a rename are restored; only the quoting is unhandled.\n- **The header finding is not a behaviour bug.** It costs maintainer time, not a wrong run, which is why it stays P2.\n\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\n\n- The restore's HEAD-versus-index rule: a path HEAD knows is checked out from HEAD, a path HEAD does not know loses its index entry and its file.\n- The pre-turn snapshot rule that never reverts a path inherited from another worker.\n- The per-arm summary's printed counts and the `policy` lines; the README's field list matches them.\n- The legacy parity test's 195-row corpus and its accepted-divergence list (unchanged since pass one; no new contradiction found).\n- `measure-embedder-cost.mjs`'s README sentence: the run spawns the advisor CLI but starts no model server, and the README says the model server is the subject.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- **A restore gap for staged new files.** Ruled out: `restorePath` drops the index entry and deletes the file, and the test covers it.\n- **A rename restored on one side only.** Ruled out: both sides of the porcelain ` -> ` pair are keys.\n- **The legacy parity test's accepted list contradicting its comment.** Ruled out: the list is explicitly \"reviewed-accepted top-1 divergence\", so `rr-iter3-105`'s presence matches \"accepted as a divergence, not as correct\".\n- **The legacy parity test's expectation history missing a step.** Ruled out: the final sentence records the `rr-iter3-076` move that takes the pair from 116/111 to 117/112.\n- **`measure-embedder-cost.mjs` claiming it starts nothing while spawning the CLI.** Ruled out: the claim is about the model server, and the script starts none.\n\n## Next Focus\n\n- Dimension: cross-cutting stabilization\n- Focus area: re-read the two P2 findings' lines, re-run the two guard test files, and confirm every dimension has at least one full pass before the loop's last iteration\n- Reason: all four dimensions are covered; one stabilization pass is required before a legal stop\n- Rotation status: D1-D4 complete; stabilization pass next\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive (restore probe, header comparison); blocked (no live arm)\n- Required evidence: the findings still active at their cited lines, and no new defect in the areas already covered\n\nReview verdict: PASS\n","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/accuracy-runner-2/iterations/iteration-005.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/accuracy-runner-2/iterations/iteration-005.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/accuracy-runner-2/iterations/iteration-005.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\":\"# Review Iteration 005\\n\\n## Dispatcher\\n\\n- Mode: review (fan-out lineage `accuracy-runner-2`, pass two)\\n- Session: `fanout-accuracy-runner-2-1794570021136-noekp0`, generation 1, lineage mode `new`\\n- Dimension: D4 Maintainability\\n- Review target: `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build` (spec-folder)\\n- Budget profile: verify (11 review actions)\\n- Operator review focus bound as `review_focus`: pass-two review of command surfacing in the `accuracy-runner` area; look for new defects in the rest of the area, including the files not changed since pass one, and check that their contracts still describe what they do.\\n- How this iteration addressed the focus: it read the restore helper end to end (a pass-one clean surface) and probed `git status --porcelain` quoting in a throwaway repository inside the lineage, then re-read the scorer's module header against the summary it actually prints. Two P2s: a restore path that cannot act on a quoted file name, and a header that promises an ordered read/write report the CLI never emits.\\n- `steer.md`: absent before this iteration; no lead rulings to apply.\\n\\n## Files Reviewed\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/worktree-restore.mjs:1-122`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs:1140-1189`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1-43,1247-1280,1392-1427`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/worktree-restore.vitest.ts:1-122`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md:100-125`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-corpus-parity.vitest.ts:1-198`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/legacy/advisor-observability.vitest.ts` (health assertions only)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/measure-embedder-cost.mjs` (README claim only)\\n\\n## Findings - New\\n\\n### P0 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P1 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P2 Findings\\n\\n1. **A file name git quotes cannot be restored, so one space in a created path stops the arm and leaves the file** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/worktree-restore.mjs:54` -- `statusSnapshot` reads `git status --porcelain` and keys each path with `line.slice(3)`. Git quotes a path that contains a space (probe inside the lineage: `?? \\\"a b.txt\\\"`), so the map key is `\\\"a b.txt\\\"` including both quote characters. `restorePath` then asks `git cat-file -e 'HEAD:\\\"a b.txt\\\"'`, which is not a path git knows; `git rm --cached --ignore-unmatch -- '\\\"a b.txt\\\"'` also matches nothing; and `rmSync(resolve(cwd, '\\\"a b.txt\\\"'))` deletes a differently named file at most. The turn's file therefore stays, `leftoverPaths` reports the quoted key, and `restoreTurn` returns `restored: false`, so `runTurn` ends the arm with `restore_failed`. The failure is safe but unrecoverable by the runner: the next live arm refuses `worktree_dirty` on the leftover until someone deletes it by hand.\\nFinding class: correctness / restore-completeness\\nScope proof: the finding is inside the restore helper and its only caller; the probe wrote only inside the lineage's `logs/quotepath-probe` directory.\\nAffected surface hints: `worktree-restore.mjs` `statusSnapshot` and `restorePath`; `run-command-behaviour.mjs` `runTurn`'s `restore_failed` branch; `tests/worktree-restore.vitest.ts`.\\nCase: a measured turn writes `notes draft.md` in the arena root; `git status --porcelain` prints `?? \\\"notes draft.md\\\"`; after the turn the restore leaves the file, reports `restore_failed` with `paths: [\\\"\\\\\\\"notes draft.md\\\\\\\"\\\"]`, and the next arm refuses `worktree_dirty` until the operator removes it.\\n\\n2. **The transcript scorer's header promises an ordered read/write report the CLI does not print** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:5` -- The header says the tool \\\"reports what a model did with the commands a turn offered it: the `COMMAND:` lines it printed, the reads and writes in order, and whether a started loop ran its init first.\\\" The CLI has two outputs: `--summary` prints per-arm counts (`writeSummary`, line 1392-1427) and `--runtime --locator` prints a record count (`scoreSession`, line 885-901). The ordered read/write stream exists only inside `normalize` as an internal event list; no mode prints it. A maintainer looking for the ordered report the header names will search for an output path that does not exist, and may add a third mode instead of using `normalize` directly.\\nFinding class: maintainability / documentation drift\\nScope proof: the header and both output modes are in the declared area.\\nAffected surface hints: `score-command-transcripts.mjs` module header and `writeSummary`; any future maintainer adding a per-session report.\\nCase: a maintainer needs the write order for one session and reads the header; they expect the CLI to print it, find only counts and unscored reasons, and either add a redundant printer or read `normalize` from a test harness.\\n\\n## Traceability Checks\\n\\n- `spec_code`: pass. No new number claim was made this iteration; the five floors re-derived in iteration 4 still hold.\\n- `checklist_evidence`: partial. No parent checklist; phase 007's open rows remain.\\n- `skill_agent`: notApplicable.\\n- `agent_cross_runtime`: partial. The restore defect is runtime-independent; the per-runtime proof stays phase 007's.\\n- `feature_catalog_code`: pass (carried from iteration 4).\\n- `playbook_capability`: partial (root playbook statements carry iteration 4's findings; NC-011 holds).\\n\\n## Integration Evidence\\n\\n- `worktree-restore.mjs` ↔ `run-command-behaviour.mjs`: `runTurn` treats `restored: false` as `restore_failed` and stops the arm, which is why the quoted-path defect surfaces as a blocked arm rather than a silent leftover.\\n- `worktree-restore.mjs` ↔ `tests/worktree-restore.vitest.ts`: the test covers staged, deleted and inherited paths but no path with a space or a quote, which is why the defect survives a green suite.\\n- `score-command-transcripts.mjs` ↔ `README.md:112`: the README describes the counts the CLI prints, while the module header describes a stream; the README is the accurate of the two.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- **The probe was local and minimal.** A throwaway `git init` inside the lineage's `logs/` folder showed the quoting for a space and a quote; it wrote nothing outside the lineage and was removed afterwards.\\n- **The restore still fails safe.** The leftover is reported and the arm stops, so no wrong score is recorded; the cost is that a legitimate write blocks the next arm until a human intervenes.\\n- **A renamed path is handled.** `statusSnapshot` splits on ` -> `, so both sides of a rename are restored; only the quoting is unhandled.\\n- **The header finding is not a behaviour bug.** It costs maintainer time, not a wrong run, which is why it stays P2.\\n\\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\\n\\n- The restore's HEAD-versus-index rule: a path HEAD knows is checked out from HEAD, a path HEAD does not know loses its index entry and its file.\\n- The pre-turn snapshot rule that never reverts a path inherited from another worker.\\n- The per-arm summary's printed counts and the `policy` lines; the README's field list matches them.\\n- The legacy parity test's 195-row corpus and its accepted-divergence list (unchanged since pass one; no new contradiction found).\\n- `measure-embedder-cost.mjs`'s README sentence: the run spawns the advisor CLI but starts no model server, and the README says the model server is the subject.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- **A restore gap for staged new files.** Ruled out: `restorePath` drops the index entry and deletes the file, and the test covers it.\\n- **A rename restored on one side only.** Ruled out: both sides of the porcelain ` -> ` pair are keys.\\n- **The legacy parity test's accepted list contradicting its comment.** Ruled out: the list is explicitly \\\"reviewed-accepted top-1 divergence\\\", so `rr-iter3-105`'s presence matches \\\"accepted as a divergence, not as correct\\\".\\n- **The legacy parity test's expectation history missing a step.** Ruled out: the final sentence records the `rr-iter3-076` move that takes the pair from 116/111 to 117/112.\\n- **`measure-embedder-cost.mjs` claiming it starts nothing while spawning the CLI.** Ruled out: the claim is about the model server, and the script starts none.\\n\\n## Next Focus\\n\\n- Dimension: cross-cutting stabilization\\n- Focus area: re-read the two P2 findings' lines, re-run the two guard test files, and confirm every dimension has at least one full pass before the loop's last iteration\\n- Reason: all four dimensions are covered; one stabilization pass is required before a legal stop\\n- Rotation status: D1-D4 complete; stabilization pass next\\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive (restore probe, header comparison); blocked (no live arm)\\n- Required evidence: the findings still active at their cited lines, and no new defect in the areas already covered\\n\\nReview verdict: PASS\\n\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/accuracy-runner-2/iterations/iteration-005.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794572011811,"end":1794572034633}}}}
